WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:08.607
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.907 --> 00:00:16.981
Unit nine: The Psychology of Decision-Making. In this unit, you will understand the hidden processes behind our choices.

4
00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:19.054
heuristic.

5
00:00:19.754 --> 00:00:23.449
heuristic means a mental shortcut used to make quick judgements.

6
00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:26.549
Heuristics save time but can lead to errors.

7
00:00:27.249 --> 00:00:30.285
Your turn. Say: heuristic.

8
00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:33.939
heuristic.

9
00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.485
bounded rationality.

10
00:00:37.185 --> 00:00:44.479
bounded rationality means the idea that our rational choices are limited by time and information.

11
00:00:44.779 --> 00:00:48.752
Bounded rationality explains why we choose 'good enough' options.

12
00:00:49.452 --> 00:00:52.604
Your turn. Say: bounded rationality.

13
00:00:55.304 --> 00:00:56.897
bounded rationality.

14
00:00:57.883 --> 00:00:59.210
prospect theory.

15
00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:05.938
prospect theory means a theory showing that people value losses and gains differently.

16
00:01:06.238 --> 00:01:09.398
Prospect theory won Kahneman a Nobel Prize.

17
00:01:10.098 --> 00:01:13.412
Your turn. Say: prospect theory.

18
00:01:16.112 --> 00:01:17.253
prospect theory.

19
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.541
choice overload.

20
00:01:20.241 --> 00:01:25.085
choice overload means difficulty deciding when there are too many options.

21
00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:28.569
Choice overload can make shoppers leave without buying.

22
00:01:29.269 --> 00:01:32.502
Your turn. Say: choice overload.

23
00:01:35.202 --> 00:01:36.540
choice overload.

24
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.074
status quo bias.

25
00:01:39.774 --> 00:01:43.874
status quo bias means a preference for keeping things as they are.

26
00:01:44.174 --> 00:01:48.531
Status quo bias keeps people on the same phone plan for years.

27
00:01:49.231 --> 00:01:52.301
Your turn. Say: status quo bias.

28
00:01:55.001 --> 00:01:56.513
status quo bias.

29
00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.435
availability heuristic.

30
00:02:00.135 --> 00:02:05.652
availability heuristic means judging likelihood by how easily examples come to mind.

31
00:02:05.952 --> 00:02:11.772
After seeing news of a crash, the availability heuristic makes flying seem dangerous.

32
00:02:12.472 --> 00:02:15.995
Your turn. Say: availability heuristic.

33
00:02:18.695 --> 00:02:20.671
availability heuristic.

34
00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:23.230
dual-process theory.

35
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:30.353
dual-process theory means the idea that we think with a fast intuitive system and a slow deliberate one.

36
00:02:30.653 --> 00:02:34.173
Dual-process theory distinguishes System 1 and System 2.

37
00:02:34.873 --> 00:02:37.886
Your turn. Say: dual-process theory.

38
00:02:40.586 --> 00:02:42.191
dual-process theory.

39
00:02:43.183 --> 00:02:44.219
intuition.

40
00:02:44.919 --> 00:02:49.705
intuition means understanding something instantly without conscious reasoning.

41
00:02:50.005 --> 00:02:53.050
Experienced doctors often rely on intuition.

42
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.565
Your turn. Say: intuition.

43
00:02:59.265 --> 00:03:00.126
intuition.

44
00:03:01.117 --> 00:03:03.360
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.459
Rithy, do you trust your gut when you make big decisions?

46
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:13.469
Sometimes. But I've learned not to take my intuition at face value.

47
00:03:14.783 --> 00:03:20.129
Wise. Kahneman's dual-process theory says our fast system often jumps the gun.

48
00:03:21.417 --> 00:03:26.136
Like the availability heuristic? One scary headline and we're afraid of flying.

49
00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.866
Exactly. And when there are fifty options, choice overload leaves us stuck in a rut.

50
00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:38.019
So we stick with the status quo, even when it's a raw deal.

51
00:03:39.317 --> 00:03:42.849
Prospect theory adds that losses loom larger than gains.

52
00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:48.355
Then the trick is to slow down and weigh things up before taking the plunge.

53
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.811
Grammar focus: Idioms and figurative language.

54
00:03:53.111 --> 00:03:56.387
Idioms and figurative language add colour at C2.

55
00:03:56.687 --> 00:04:02.883
Don't take your intuition at face value. We get stuck in a rut. Losses loom larger than gains.

56
00:04:03.183 --> 00:04:05.926
Use them naturally and sparingly.

57
00:04:06.226 --> 00:04:13.554
C2 speakers use idioms naturally but sparingly; one well-chosen idiom is more effective than five.

58
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.527
Now read and listen. Thinking, Fast and Slow.

59
00:04:17.827 --> 00:04:23.113
Psychologist Daniel Kahneman described the mind as operating through two systems.

60
00:04:24.417 --> 00:04:30.851
System 1 is fast, intuitive and effortless; System 2 is slow, deliberate and demanding.

61
00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:38.329
Most of the time, System 1 serves us well, relying on heuristics to make quick judgements.

62
00:04:39.617 --> 00:04:46.260
Yet these shortcuts can mislead us, as the availability heuristic and status quo bias demonstrate.

63
00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:53.868
Herbert Simon's concept of bounded rationality reminds us that we rarely have full information.

64
00:04:55.183 --> 00:05:00.666
Prospect theory shows that the pain of a loss looms larger than the pleasure of an equal gain.

65
00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:08.013
Choice overload, meanwhile, can paralyse decision-making altogether.

66
00:05:09.317 --> 00:05:16.309
Recognising these patterns does not eliminate them, but it allows us to pause before taking the plunge.

67
00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:20.304
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:20.604 --> 00:05:22.603
A mental shortcut is a blank.

69
00:05:22.903 --> 00:05:28.496
A, heuristic. B, prospect theory. C, intuition.

70
00:05:32.483 --> 00:05:35.063
The answer is A: heuristic.

71
00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:38.480
To act too early is to blank.

72
00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:43.827
A, jump the gun. B, take the plunge. C, loom larger.

73
00:05:47.817 --> 00:05:50.188
The answer is A: jump the gun.

74
00:05:51.683 --> 00:05:55.970
If you accept something without question, you take it at blank value.

75
00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.436
A, face. B, full. C, true.

76
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.111
The answer is A: face.

77
00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:09.976
Preferring to keep things as they are is blank.

78
00:06:10.276 --> 00:06:16.577
A, status quo bias. B, choice overload. C, bounded rationality.

79
00:06:20.583 --> 00:06:23.523
The answer is A: status quo bias.

80
00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:28.885
According to the text, which system is slow and deliberate?

81
00:06:29.185 --> 00:06:33.222
A, System 1. B, System 2. C, both.

82
00:06:37.217 --> 00:06:39.808
The answer is B: System 2.

83
00:06:41.317 --> 00:06:48.776
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should we trust intuition or careful analysis when making important decisions?

84
00:06:54.076 --> 00:07:00.150
Part two: Use two idioms; refer to one cognitive bias in your answer.

85
00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:20.413
Both have their place. Intuition is valuable for experts, but taking it at face value is risky. The availability heuristic, for instance, can make rare dangers loom larger than they are, so big decisions deserve slow analysis.

86
00:07:20.713 --> 00:07:23.224
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:24.217 --> 00:07:29.713
Great work today! You learned eight new words and idioms and figurative language.

88
00:07:30.013 --> 00:07:33.629
Today's value is good habits. Do good things every day.

89
00:07:33.929 --> 00:07:36.350
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

90
00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.395
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

